WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.139 --> 00:00:06.220
Hey youtubers it’s Charlie, hopefully you’ve
had a chance to see the Hobbit this week.

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:07.520
This is going to be my review but since a
lot of you probably haven’t I’ll start

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:11.049
with a non spoilery part and then get into
specific moments.

00:00:11.049 --> 00:00:14.820
I’m a huge Peter Jackson fan and even though
it’s been a long long time since I’ve

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:18.000
read any Tolkien, I’m a huge fan of the
books and the films.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.640
I still think the animated Hobbit Movie is
one of the scariest cartoons I ever saw as

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:23.640
a child.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.560
That’s a much more faithful adaptation of
the original novel.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:34.590
Which is the shortest Tolkien book by far.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:39.350
And one of the only ones, specifically intended
for little children.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:42.870
Being that this movie was Part 3, I went in
thinking about that.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:47.500
I totally didn’t mind that they took a short
book and turned it into a 7 plus hour movie

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:49.809
but I feel like when they release it on DVD.

00:00:49.809 --> 00:00:51.859
That’s how they need to market it.

00:00:51.859 --> 00:00:53.470
As one really long movie.

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:59.091
Because there is no way you will understand
the what or the why of the action of this

00:00:59.091 --> 00:01:00.809
movie without watching the first two movies.

00:01:00.809 --> 00:01:03.120
I saw both of those, so no problems there.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:07.490
But I consider it a big thumbs down that the
movie can’t stand by itself.

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:09.670
For example look at the Lord of the Rings
trilogy.

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:12.159
Return of the King was a much stronger narrative.

00:01:12.159 --> 00:01:15.689
You could watch that movie by itself and it
would still feel like it had a beginning,

00:01:15.689 --> 00:01:17.020
middle and an end.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:25.460
The Hobbit Part 3 feels like it was taken
out of Peter Jackson’s extended cut of the

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:27.399
second film, Desolation of Smaug.

00:01:27.399 --> 00:01:31.580
Like this would have worked a lot better if
Part 2 had been a 4 hour movie and they’d

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:33.350
have used this as the third act.

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:37.740
Find the mountain, defeat the dragon, battle
the other armies and take a hobbit nap for

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:41.869
about a hundred years till Gandalf comes to
take Frodo on an adventure.

00:01:41.869 --> 00:01:46.780
All those issues aside, the movie was classic
Peter Jackson Middle Earth.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.860
Martin Freeman and Richard Armitage were amazing.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:50.469
They were the real stars of the film.

00:01:50.469 --> 00:01:55.250
Ian McKellen as Gandalaf has a big role as
does Lee Pace, Evangeline Lily and Orlando

00:01:55.250 --> 00:02:00.680
Bloom.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:15.420
But the core of the story is about the friendship
between Bilbo and Thorin.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:20.270
And that breaks through what is mostly a two
hour extended action scene.

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:22.780
Remember the Battle for Gondor in Return of
The King.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.260
Imagine the whole movie is just that battle.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:28.613
Normally that would be a thumbs down, but
because I’m trying to think ahead and think

00:02:28.613 --> 00:02:30.780
of this as a 7 or 8 hour movie.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.459
It works fine in that context.

00:02:32.459 --> 00:02:34.250
There’s just not a whole lot of story.

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:39.431
And what story there is, mostly serves to
tee up the events of the first Lord of The

00:02:39.431 --> 00:02:41.470
Rings movie, Fellowship.

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:46.220
There is a seamless transition between this
movie and Fellowship that everyone will enjoy.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:51.400
So if you ever find yourself with like 20
hours to kill, you can do the Star Wars Prequels/Original

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.980
Trilogy treatment, where you watch all six
films in chronological order.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:57.770
Just make sure to do the Extended Editions,
because that’s how it’s done.

00:02:57.770 --> 00:03:00.180
I think the last time I actually had time
like that was during my College Summer Breaks.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:04.290
Overall, I gave the movie a B plus for the
sheer weight of accomplishment and that it

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:08.700
works so well when you look at it as the third
act of the second film.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:13.120
I would have given it an A, but I had to thumbs
down the lack of story.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.800
It just didn’t have enough to exist without
those first two films.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:20.269
I will definitely be buying the extended edition
blu rays.

00:03:20.269 --> 00:03:22.931
Peter Jackson is always one of the best when
it comes to bonus features.

00:03:22.931 --> 00:03:23.931
Sometimes I just with they could release bonus
features without the actual movies.

00:03:23.931 --> 00:03:26.280
If I’ve already seen a movie, I care more
about what the Director and Cast have to say

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.330
about it than just re-watching it over and
over.

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:33.680
Now I’m going to get into my favorite moments
and a slightly more detailed analysis.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:36.590
So careful for spoilers if you haven’t seen
it yet.

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:37.860
Starting with Top 5 moments:

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.189
Number 5 - Bard Kills Smaug

00:03:40.189 --> 00:03:43.260
This definitely should have been the end of
the second movie, but it was such a huge deal

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:47.420
and it tees up an almost Return of The King
type moment where Bard takes up leadership

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:48.420
of Dale.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:52.350
In the canon he does become the first king
of what you can consider New Dale in the events

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:57.060
following the movie.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:01.900
Benedict Cumberbatch was barely in this movie
and I was watching it in 3D, so the action

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:06.080
was a little out of focus and there were some
brightness issues.

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:09.390
But that had nothing to do with the film.

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:12.000
I could have done without his son.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:19.580
I feel like parts of this movie were made
for little children.

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:27.920
Like anytime the camera followed his son it
just slowed the action down so much.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:32.080
But Hollywood caters to children, so I think
that, in addition to the Hobbit Source material

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:35.970
being a children’s book, is why they added
the extra children scenes.

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:38.320
Number 4 - Thranduil versus Everyone

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:41.200
So happy to get a ton of Lee Pace this time.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:43.580
He is like the ultimate emo elf.

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:48.680
If Martin Freeman flipping people off in his
Bilbo makeup isn’t enough to crash tumblr.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:52.770
Lee Pace in his Thranduil makeup will finish
the job.

00:04:52.770 --> 00:05:03.630
As a 
dramatic arc, there’s not a lot here for

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:08.430
his character, other than to tie up a minor
story teased in the prologue of the first

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:09.430
film.

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:10.960
Get back the gems of pure starlight.

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:16.730
This is one of the big easter eggs of the
movie.

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:28.190
And the conflict that Thranduil is referencing
goes all the way back to Thingol, who was

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:29.590
the first Sindarian King.

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:32.830
They’re the group of elves that Thranduil
belongs to.

00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:39.200
There were a lot of different groups of Elves
and all of them had their own cultures.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.970
They’re mostly delineated by three big groups.

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:44.110
But it gets way more complicated than that.

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:49.240
The reason The Dwarves and the Sindar feuded
was over payment for a very famous necklace

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:50.680
called Naglaumir.

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:53.000
It was made of those white gems.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:58.080
The dwarves constructed it and later Thingol,
High King of the elves had one of the simarils

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:00.190
added to it.

00:06:00.190 --> 00:06:09.320
I know everyone is asking about a Simarillion
tv series.

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:15.240
The dwarves felt like the elves didn’t give
them fair payment.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:20.840
The necklace was the finest piece of dwarven
craftsmanship during that age.

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:21.840
Thus the feud began.

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:23.250
And continued to where we are right now.

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:25.470
That’s like the super short version of it.

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:26.790
Number 3 - Bilbo and Thorin

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:30.930
They are the emotional core of the film, I
would argue the most important part.

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:33.270
If not for them, there would have been no
movie.

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:35.860
Martin Freeman and Richard Armitage were amazing.

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:37.840
Thorin going crazy was a lot of fun to watch.

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:39.830
I kept wondering how far they’d push it.

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:43.660
Like how far over the top they’d make his
hallucinations.

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:47.020
The Gold Sucking him under was pretty trippy,
and I did laugh a little.

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:48.340
It got a little silly.

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:52.230
But so much fun watching their back and forth,
especially before Bilbo decides to tell him

00:06:52.230 --> 00:07:01.030
about 
the Arkenstone.

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:03.330
Number 2 - Galadriel Defeats Sauron

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:05.410
This is almost like a tie with my number one
moment.

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:08.970
Back when the second film had already come
out and Peter Jackson was talking about Part

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:09.970
3.

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:12.940
He said that we’d get to see Galadriel lose
her shit.

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:15.160
Imagine that being said with Peter Jackson’s
voice.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:16.160
It just sounds so funny.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:18.060
So I knew that we’d get a Galadriel battle
scene.

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:19.870
But I didn’t know how it would go down.

00:07:19.870 --> 00:07:22.880
I loved the Sauron fight, but some of the
CG action was a little too crazy.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:23.880
It just didn’t feel very real to me.

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:27.440
So out of that whole sequence of events, I
just focus on Galadriel beating Sauron back.

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:29.820
It gives you a good sense of the scope of
her powers.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:32.020
And just to give you something to compare
it to.

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:33.300
The Elven rings of power.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:35.740
The three given to them by Sauron.

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:40.100
Elrond is the keeper of the most powerful
one.

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:44.150
Galadriel has one and Gandalf has the other.

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:50.330
They don’t make a big deal about it.

00:07:50.330 --> 00:07:54.410
The rings enhance some of their abilities,
but they all have different properties.

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:58.060
Galadriel is still the most powerful person
in Middle earth next to Sauron.

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:03.120
And for those asking about the rings.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:05.910
When the One Ring was destroyed in the fires
of mount doom.

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:08.000
The power faded from all the other rings.

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:12.850
Number 1 - The Young Bilbo transition to Lord
of the Rings Bilbo

00:08:12.850 --> 00:08:17.580
This wasn’t the most eye gouging intense
moment, but the way Peter jackson cut the

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:23.370
film makes it perfect for your bookshelf and
marathon viewing.

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:29.550
So later, when we’re all watching these
films for the billionth time.

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:35.240
They’ll all work as one continuous narrative
with no significant act breaks.

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:43.669
And this is the kind of epic fantasy series
that’s mean to be re-read, re-watched several

00:08:43.669 --> 00:08:44.669
times.

00:08:44.669 --> 00:08:45.810
Which I will begin doing right now.

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:50.920
Let me know though, what was your favorite
part of the movie and do you want to see Peter

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:54.380
Jackson or someone else try to adapt the Simarillion
as a television series.

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:57.270
It would be the events of the war of the jewels.

00:08:57.270 --> 00:08:58.270
In and around that.

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:04.710
So about 2400 years before the events of this
movie.

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:10.890
It was after that war that Thranduil’s father
came west to establish the kingdom of the

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:11.890
Sindar Elves.

00:09:11.890 --> 00:10:11.740
That’s how long ago it was.

